{0 Cache}

{!Workero.Cache} is the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/cache/}Cache API} of
the data center that runs the Worker ([caches.default]). It needs no binding.
Entries are keyed by URL string, and each data center has its own cache.

{@ocaml[
let url = "https://example.com/data.json"

let handler _req _env ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match Cache.match_ url with
  | Some cached -> cached
  | None ->
      let upstream = fetch url in
      let body = Response.body_bytes upstream in
      let headers = [ ("cache-control", "max-age=60") ] in
      Context.wait_until ctx (fun () ->
          Cache.put url (Response.make ~headers body));
      Response.make ~headers body

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- {!Workero.Cache.match_} gives [Some response] for a URL, or [None].
- {!Workero.Cache.put} caches a response. The runtime reads the body of the
  response you give it, so send the client a different {!Workero.Response.t}
  made from the same bytes, as the sample does. It rejects a response with
  status 206 or a [Vary: *] header. It keeps the entry only as long as the
  [cache-control] header allows, so set [max-age].
- {!Workero.Cache.delete} removes an entry. It gives [false] when there was
  none.

The cache is not shared between data centers. On a [*.workers.dev] domain, the
Cache API does not store anything. Use a
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/routing/custom-domains/}custom domain}
to see it work in production. In [wrangler dev], it works locally.
